    43  /**
    44   * Rate limiting filter, with configurable threshold above which 
non-privileged clients will be
    45   * dropped. This feature enables cutting off non-standard and 
potentially harmful clients in case of
    46   * system overload. Since it is critical to always allow client 
registrations and heartbeats into
    47   * the system, which at the same time are relatively cheap operations, 
the rate limiting is applied
    48   * only to full and delta registry fetches. Furthermore, since delta 
fetches are much smaller than
    49   * full fetches, and if not served my result in following full registry 
fetch from the client, they
    50   * have relatively higher priority. This is implemented by two parallel 
rate limiters, one for
    51   * overall number of full/delta fetches (higher threshold) and one for 
full fetches only (low
    52   * threshold).
    53   *
    54   * <p>The client is identified by {@link 
AbstractEurekaIdentity#AUTH_NAME_HEADER_KEY} HTTP header
    55   * value. The privileged group by default contains:
    56   *
    57   * <ul>
    58   *   <li>{@link EurekaClientIdentity#DEFAULT_CLIENT_NAME} - standard 
Java eureka-client.
    59   *       Applications using this client automatically belong to the 
privileged group.
    60   *   <li>{@link 
com.netflix.eureka.EurekaServerIdentity#DEFAULT_SERVER_NAME} - connections from 
peer
    61   *       Eureka servers (internal only, traffic replication)
    62   * </ul>
    63   *
    64   * It is possible to turn off privileged client filtering via {@link
    65   * EurekaServerConfig#isRateLimiterThrottleStandardClients()} property.
    66   *
    67   * <p>Rate limiting is not enabled by default, but can be turned on via 
configuration. Even when
    68   * disabled, the throttling statistics are still counted, although on a 
separate counter, so it is
    69   * possible to measure the impact of this feature before activation.
    70   *
    71   * <p>Rate limiter implementation is based on token bucket algorithm. 
There are two configurable
    72   * parameters:
    73   *
    74   * <ul>
    75   *   <li>burst size - maximum number of requests allowed into the system
as a burst
    76   *   <li>average rate - expected number of requests per second
    77   * </ul>
    78   *
    79   * @author Tomasz Bak
    80   */
